Chapter: BrowserChromeLayoutTest
  cites Browser chapter Browser
  cites Browser chapter BrowserTheme
  cites Browser chapter Display
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Layout
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The browser's chrome asked for a 32-pixel tab bar and a 28-pixel address bar
 with `widget-set-min`, which cannot say that. `widget-panel` defaults
 `wn-flex` to 1 where `widget-label` and `widget-button` default it to 0, so
 both bars stayed flexible and a minimum they never reached bound nothing:
 the three rows split the window evenly at 247 pixels each. Every button then
 stretched to its row's height, because a row sizes its children's cross axis
 to the container, and the browser drew its nav buttons as full-height bars.

 `format-widget` is what makes this observable without a display, and it
 carries the id as well as the rect, so a row that moves and a row that is
 renamed both show up here.

Section: Test

  chrome-row : WidgetNode, Integer -> Text
  chrome-row (laid) (i) = format-widget (list-at (laid.wn-children) i)

  opening : [Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] Nothing = act
    st <- browser-init 1024 768 browser-theme-default display-init-text-fallback
    let laid = widget-layout (browser-render st) (st.bs-theme) (layout-rect 0 0 1024 768)
    in act
      print-line-uni (chrome-row laid 0)
      print-line-uni (chrome-row laid 1)
      print-line-uni (chrome-row laid 2)
    end
  end
